{"name":"Crab Nebula","hex":"#004455","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Crab Nebula","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A supernova remnant and pulsar wind nebula in the constellation of Taurus."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Crab_Nebula"}],"entries":[{"title":"Crab Nebula","blocks":[{"text":"The Crab Nebula is a supernova remnant and pulsar wind nebula in the constellation of Taurus. The common name comes from a drawing that somewhat resembled a crab with arms produced by William Parsons, 3rd Earl of Rosse, in 1842 or 1843 using a 36-inch (91 cm) telescope. The nebula was discovered by English astronomer John Bevis in 1731. It corresponds with a bright supernova observed in AD 1054 by Native American, Japanese, and Arab stargazers; this supernova was also recorded by Chinese astronomers as a guest star. The nebula was the first astronomical object identified that corresponds with a historically-observed supernova explosion."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Crab_Nebula.jpg/330px-Crab_Nebula.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crab_Nebula"},{"title":"Crab","blocks":[{"text":"Crabs are decapod crustaceans, either the Brachyura or various groups within the closely related Anomura, characterised by having a heavily armoured shell, their tail segments concealed under the body, the ability to run sideways, and the habit of hiding in rocky crevices. They do not form a single natural group or clade, but have convergently evolved multiple times from the ancestral decapod body plan through carcinisation, the process of creating this set of characteristics. As a group, they are thus polyphyletic, meaning they have multiple evolutionary origins."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Cancer_pagurus.jpg/330px-Cancer_pagurus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crab"},{"title":"Nebula","blocks":[{"text":"A nebula is a distinct luminescent part of interstellar medium, which can consist of ionized, neutral, or molecular hydrogen and also cosmic dust. Nebulae are often star-forming regions, such as the Pillars of Creation in the Eagle Nebula. In these regions, the formations of gas, dust, and other materials \"clump\" together to form denser regions, which attract further matter and eventually become dense enough to form stars. The remaining material is then thought to form planets and other planetary system objects."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Trifid_Nebula_by_Deddy_Dayag.jpg/330px-Trifid_Nebula_by_Deddy_Dayag.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nebula"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/M1rosse.jpg/330px-M1rosse.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/M1rosse.jpg/330px-M1rosse.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Reproduction of the first depiction of the nebula by Lord Rosse (1844) (colour-inverted to appear white-on-black)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AM1rosse.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/The_Crab_Nebula_M1_Goran_Nilsson_%26_The_Liverpool_Telescope.jpg/500px-The_Crab_Nebula_M1_Goran_Nilsson_%26_The_Liverpool_Telescope.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/The_Crab_Nebula_M1_Goran_Nilsson_%26_The_Liverpool_Telescope.jpg/1280px-The_Crab_Nebula_M1_Goran_Nilsson_%26_The_Liverpool_Telescope.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/The_Crab_Nebula_M1_Goran_Nilsson_%26_The_Liverpool_Telescope.jpg/500px-The_Crab_Nebula_M1_Goran_Nilsson_%26_The_Liverpool_Telescope.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"HaRGB image of the Crab Nebula from the Liverpool Telescope, exposures totalling 1.4 hours.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Crab_Nebula_M1_Goran_Nilsson_%26_The_Liverpool_Telescope.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Stack_252frames_8064s_ps-.jpg/500px-Stack_252frames_8064s_ps-.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Stack_252frames_8064s_ps-.jpg/500px-Stack_252frames_8064s_ps-.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Crab Nebula M1","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStack_252frames_8064s_ps-.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/A_whole_new_view_of_the_Crab_Nebula.jpg/500px-A_whole_new_view_of_the_Crab_Nebula.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/A_whole_new_view_of_the_Crab_Nebula.jpg/500px-A_whole_new_view_of_the_Crab_Nebula.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The nebula is seen in the visible spectrum at 550 nm (green light).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AA_whole_new_view_of_the_Crab_Nebula.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Chandra-crab.jpg/500px-Chandra-crab.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Chandra-crab.jpg/1280px-Chandra-crab.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Chandra-crab.jpg/500px-Chandra-crab.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Image combining optical data from Hubble (in red) and X-ray images from Chandra X-ray Observatory (in blue).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChandra-crab.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Filaments_in_the_Crab_Nebula.jpg/500px-Filaments_in_the_Crab_Nebula.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Filaments_in_the_Crab_Nebula.jpg/500px-Filaments_in_the_Crab_Nebula.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hubble image of a small region of the Crab Nebula, showing Rayleigh–Taylor instabilities in its intricate filamentary structure.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFilaments_in_the_Crab_Nebula.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/M1.gif/500px-M1.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/M1.gif/500px-M1.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Slow-motion video of the Crab Pulsar, taken with OES Single-Photon-Camera.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AM1.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Motion_in_the_Crab_Nebula.gif/500px-Motion_in_the_Crab_Nebula.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Motion_in_the_Crab_Nebula.gif/1280px-Motion_in_the_Crab_Nebula.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Motion_in_the_Crab_Nebula.gif/500px-Motion_in_the_Crab_Nebula.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Expansion of the wisps recorded with Hubble between September and November 2005.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMotion_in_the_Crab_Nebula.gif"}],"sounds":{},"pictures":[{"title":"Teal Blue Abstract Magical Fabric Colorful Background 10","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Teal_Blue_Abstract_Magical_Fabric_Colorful_Background_10.jpg/500px-Teal_Blue_Abstract_Magical_Fabric_Colorful_Background_10.jpg","ratio":1.7777777777777777,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Teal_Blue_Abstract_Magical_Fabric_Colorful_Background_10.jpg","share":0.2975},{"title":"Centre of the Crab Nebula in Taurus (eso9948g)","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Centre_of_the_Crab_Nebula_in_Taurus_%28eso9948g%29.jpg/500px-Centre_of_the_Crab_Nebula_in_Taurus_%28eso9948g%29.jpg","ratio":1.0098887515451174,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Centre_of_the_Crab_Nebula_in_Taurus_(eso9948g).jpg","share":0.119375},{"title":"Crab Nebula (MIRI and NIRCam image) (weic2417a)","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Crab_Nebula_%28MIRI_and_NIRCam_image%29_%28weic2417a%29.jpg/500px-Crab_Nebula_%28MIRI_and_NIRCam_image%29_%28weic2417a%29.jpg","ratio":1.1514207862981705,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crab_Nebula_(MIRI_and_NIRCam_image)_(weic2417a).jpg","share":0.049375}]}}